## Migration Analysis

Your code has three patterns that break on .NET 8:

| API | Issue on .NET 8 |
|-----|----------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9), unreliable |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` + Interrupt | Can't be woken cooperatively |

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class EventProcessor
{
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        try
        {
            foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(_cts.Token))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Normal shutdown — exit cleanly
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        _queue.Add(item);
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();           // wakes the blocked GetConsumingEnumerable
        _thread?.Join(5000);     // wait for graceful exit
        _cts.Dispose();
        _queue.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* ... */ }
}
```

## What Changed

| Before | After | Why |
|--------|-------|-----|
| `ManualResetEvent` + `lock` + `Queue` | `BlockingCollection<string>` | Thread-safe producer-consumer built-in; handles blocking and signaling |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` | Cooperatively wakes blocked `GetConsumingEnumerable` |
| `Thread.Abort()` | Removed — cancellation ensures exit | `Abort` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on .NET 8 |
| `while (true)` + manual dequeue | `GetConsumingEnumerable(token)` | Blocks until item available or token cancelled |

## Key Pitfalls

1. **`CancellationToken` doesn't interrupt `Handle(item)`** — if `Handle` is long-running, pass the token into it and check `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at safe points.

2. **Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** — leaking it causes timer/callback leaks.

3. **Race on shutdown** — items added after `Cancel()` but before `Dispose()` are lost. If you need to drain, call `_queue.CompleteAdding()` first, wait for the loop to finish, then cancel only if it doesn't exit in time:

```csharp
public void Stop()
{
    _queue.CompleteAdding();      // signal no more items
    if (!_thread?.Join(5000) ?? true)
        _cts.Cancel();           // force exit if drain takes too long
    _cts.Dispose();
    _queue.Dispose();
}
```

4. **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException` deeper in the stack** — only catch it at the top-level loop boundary.
